# Territorial.io Architecture, Gameplay Systems, and Reverse Engineering Specification
## Operational Modes and Match Environments
Territorial.io organizes gameplay across several distinct match environments, each defined by specialized win conditions, participant caps, diplomatic constraints, and resource mechanics. For an offline engine reconstruction, these environments must be parameterized to replicate the original rules rather than relying on generic real-time strategy conventions.
| Game Mode | Entity Capacity | Diplomatic Framework | Full-Send Allowance | Primary Win Condition | Dominant Mechanics |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| **Battle Royale (Standard FFA)** | Up to 500 Entities | Informal Non-Aggression Pacts | Enabled (0% to 100%) | 100% Map Conquest or Peace Ratification | Free expansion, unconstrained offensive strikes, peace sniping |
| **Battle Royale (No Full-Send)** | Up to 500 Entities | Informal Non-Aggression Pacts | Disabled (Clamped Slider) | 100% Map Conquest or Peace Ratification | Attrition-based expansion, protected borders, long-term compounding |
| **1v1 Competitive** | Exactly 2 Entities | Strict Adversarial (No Pacts) | Enabled (0% to 100%) | Elimination or Peace Referendum | Neutral bot micro, spawn equity, rapid cutoff vectors |
| **Multi-Team Confrontation** | 100 to 500 Entities (2–8 Teams) | Rigid Colored Factions | Enabled (0% to 100%) | Annihilation of All Opposing Factions | Friendly-fire lock, taxed balance donation, coordinated pushes |
| **Zombie / Infection Mode** | Asymmetric (Humans vs. AI) | Binary: Resistance vs. Horde | Enabled (Full-strength Zombie AI) | Total Eradication of Zombie Faction | Fast zombie tick rates, high-capacity Necromancers, instant land conversion |
In the standard Free-For-All Battle Royale mode, up to 500 entities spawn simultaneously on the map canvas. Every entity acts as an independent sovereign power. The mode allows full-sending, where a player discharges up to 100% of their balance in a single attack. This mechanic creates a high-stakes dynamic: it can breach fortified defenses or retaliate against invaders, but leaves the attacker with zero balance and defenseless against nearby opportunists.
The No Full-Send Battle Royale variant applies a mathematical ceiling to the deployment slider, preventing sudden balance dumps. By restricting maximum attack allocations to lower fractions of total reserves, the mode prevents early mutual destruction and rewards careful macro-economic compounding over aggressive gambits.
The 1v1 Competitive mode is designed for direct head-to-head play on symmetrical maps such as White Arena or standardized continental layouts. The map is populated by two players surrounded by low-aggression neutral bots. Success depends on opening efficiency, path optimization through neutral space, and securing borders before engaging the opponent directly.
Multi-Team modes divide the player pool into two to eight colored factions. The engine enforces friendly-fire protection across shared borders, preventing members of the same faction from attacking or annexing allied pixels. Teammates can also transfer troops directly using a dedicated donation action subject to an overhead transaction tax. Victory is achieved when all opposing teams are eliminated, and all surviving members of the winning team share the victory.
Zombie Mode, introduced in version 1.76, provides an asymmetric cooperative experience where human players join the White Resistance to fight the automated Black Zombie Horde. The zombie faction operates with specialized economic rules: zombie territories gain balance at accelerated intervals, expand aggressively, and include high-capacity boss units (Necromancers or Over-Zombies) that automatically distribute surplus balance to frontline troops. When a zombie captures human territory, the pixels immediately convert to the horde, requiring coordinated defense and resource sharing from the human team.
## Spatial Representation, Map Topology, and Raster Memory Architecture
Territorial.io represents its game world using a discrete 2D raster grid rather than vector polygons or node graphs. Gameplay state is stored directly in indexed byte buffers that mirror the display canvas, ensuring that territory size, borders, and spatial interactions can be calculated efficiently.
| Architectural Layer | Memory Representation | Data Structure & Precision | Operational Gameplay Responsibility |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Visual Terrain Canvas** | RGBA Pixel Buffer | 32-bit Packed Integer Array (Uint32Array) | Visual rendering of biomes, relief shading, and cosmetics; no impact on traversal |
| **Playable Land Mask** | Binary Spatial Mask | 8-bit Unsigned Byte Array (Uint8Array) | Identifies traversable land (0xFF), open water (0x00), and impassable barriers (0x7F) |
| **Ownership Grid** | Indexed Entity Array | 16-bit Unsigned Integer Array (Uint16Array) | Tracks pixel ownership (0 = \text{neutral}, 1 \dots N = \text{Entity ID}) |
| **Frontier Perimeter Buffer** | Sparse Coordinate Set | Ring-buffer of 32-bit Packed (X, Y) Coordinates | Tracks active borders for expansion propagation, combat lines, and naval launches |
The engine stores the ownership grid as a flat array of size \text{Width} \times \text{Height}. A pixel coordinate (x, y) maps directly to index i = y \times \text{Width} + x. The land mask uses three primary flags: LAND, WATER, and MOUNTAIN. LAND pixels can be claimed and annexed. WATER pixels cannot be claimed, but serve as navigable space for naval units. MOUNTAIN pixels are impassable terrain obstacles that block normal ground movement, but allow instant mining attacks across shared mountain borders.
Territory ownership does not require geometric continuity. An entity's domain is tracked as the set of all pixels matching its unique ID, regardless of whether they form a single territory, multiple enclaves, or isolated islands. The engine measures territory size L_p as the total count of owned pixels:
Adjacency is calculated using orthogonal 4-way von Neumann neighborhoods or complete 8-way Moore neighborhoods on the raster grid. A pixel is considered a border pixel if at least one adjacent pixel has a different ownership ID. An entity's defensive strength is pooled globally across all owned land: an attack on a remote island enclave draws from the same total balance pool as an attack on the mainland. However, the speed of land loss depends on local border shape, since more exposed perimeter pixels allow faster enemy penetration.
## Macro-Economy: Balance, Interest, and Growth Dynamics
An entity's economic capacity, offensive power, and defensive resistance are represented by a single unified resource: **Balance** (B_p). Balance updates across three nested time intervals: sub-tick mini-ticks, interest ticks, and income cycles.
```
Mini-Ti[span_157](start_span)[span_157](end_span)[span_240](start_span)[span_240](end_span)ck: 0.056s (Conquest frontlines, boat movement, collision checks)
    └── Tick: 0.560s / 10 Mini-Ticks (Compound interest, soft/hard cap enforcement)
         └── Cycle: 5.600s / 10 Ticks (Territorial rent disbursement: +1 Balance/Pixel)

```
The fundamental simulation sub-interval is the mini-tick, which runs at t_{\text{mini}} = 0.056\text{ seconds} (56\text{ ms}). Frontline expansion waves, boat movement vectors, and pixel transfers update on this frequency.
Compound interest is calculated and applied on the interest tick, which occurs every 10 mini-ticks (t_{\text{tick}} = 0.56\text{ seconds}).
Territorial income is disbursed on the cycle pulse, which occurs every 10 interest ticks (t_{\text{cycle}} = 5.60\text{ seconds}, or 100 mini-ticks).
